Background

UNDP is the leading United Nations organization fighting to end the injustice of poverty, inequality, and climate change. Working with our broad network of experts and partners in 170 countries, we help nations to build integrated, lasting solutions for people and planet. Learn more at undp.org or follow at @UNDP.

UNDP is the United Nations’ lead agency on international development, working in 170 countries and territories to help people build better lives while protecting the planet. For 60 years, UNDP has supported countries to make progress on the issues that matter most, with human development at the core of its mandate. Our work is built on trust and long-term partnerships with governments, communities and organizations at every level.

UNDP Indonesia works with Indonesian partners to deliver an ambitious programme, fully aligned to the national priorities of Indonesia. The strategic priorities of UNDP Indonesia through its Country Programme 2026 -2030 are encapsulated under three outcome areas:

- Human Development

- Nature, Decarbonisation and Resilience

- Economic and Digital Transformation

Operating in a Middle-Income Country brings its own set of complexities and opportunities. With 70% of the Indonesian economy directly or indirectly reliant on its natural resources, the link between nature, climate, jobs, and economic empowerment become critical. UNDP’s past experiences and support include innovative sustainable financing, digital health, energy transition and climate policies, improved service delivery that leaves no one behind. Building on its vast and long experiences, and the knowledge of its entire team of professionals, UNDP Indonesia demonstrate its thought-leadership and translate knowledge into policy advice, investment opportunities and scalable solutions for Indonesia’s critical development priorities.

UNDP Indonesia is driving a strategic portfolio to transform commodity systems and sustainable land use through high-impact partnerships and innovative blended finance. The role of the private sector is pivotal to achieve national priorities, particularly within land and marine based commodities. Businesses, producer organizations, financial institutions, commodity platforms, and industry associations play a critical role in advancing sustainable production practices, responsible sourcing, traceability, market access, investment mobilization, and the adoption of environmental, social and governance (ESG) principles. Strengthening collaboration between the public and private sectors is therefore essential to creating enabling conditions for sustainable commodity production, improving market incentives for smallholders, mobilizing additional investment and co-financing, and supporting the long-term sustainability and scalability of project interventions.

Duties and Responsibilities

The Private Sector Engagement Analyst will lead and coordinate UNDP Indonesia’s engagement with private sector to advance sustainable, inclusive and market-oriented commodity development, with a particular focus on land-based and marine-based commodities. The position will identify, develop and operationalize strategic partnerships with companies, buyers, investors, financial institutions, commodity associations, producer organizations and other market actors, with the objective of translating private sector engagement into tangible investments, market opportunities, sustainable sourcing arrangements, and improved outcomes for producers and landscapes.

The position will contribute across UNDP Indonesia’s sustainable commodity portfolio, including initiatives such as FOLUR, FAST, SLPI, GCP and other relevant programmes and projects. The incumbent will work across national, landscape and, where relevant, regional and international levels, connecting project-level interventions with broader market transformation opportunities. The scope of work is organized around four key areas:

Strategic Positioning and Partnership Development

The incumbent will strengthen UNDP Indonesia’s strategic positioning with private sector actors and build a pipeline of credible and actionable partnerships around priority commodities and landscapes. Key responsibilities include:

- Develop and maintain a strategic overview of private sector actors relevant to Indonesia’s priority land- and marine-based commodities, including multinational and domestic companies, commodity traders, processors, retailers, international buyers, financial institutions, impact investors, producer organizations, commodity associations, certification bodies and private sector coalitions;

- Identify and prioritize potential private sector partners based on their market position, sourcing footprint, investment capacity, sustainability commitments, technical capabilities, and potential contribution to priority commodity and landscape outcomes;

- Develop and maintain a pipeline of potential partnerships, investment opportunities and market-based solutions, identifying opportunities for collaboration across UNDP projects and programmes;

- Establish, maintain and expand strategic relationships with private sector actors at national, regional and international levels to mobilize technical expertise, innovation, investment, market access, co-financing and other forms of private sector contribution;

- Position UNDP Indonesia as a credible partner for private sector engagement on sustainable commodities, responsible sourcing, landscape approaches, restoration, resilient production, inclusive livelihoods and sustainable trade;

- Facilitate business dialogues, investment forums, trade missions, market outreach, knowledge exchanges and other engagement mechanisms that connect Indonesian commodity stakeholders with potential buyers, investors and other market actors;

- Strengthen linkages among UNDP’s commodity, landscape, climate, biodiversity, finance and private sector portfolios to identify opportunities for integrated partnerships and resource mobilization;

- Provide strategic advice to UNDP project teams and senior management on private sector positioning, partnership opportunities, market trends and emerging opportunities for engagement.

Follow-through and Implementation of Commodity Partnerships

The incumbent will translate strategic relationships into concrete partnerships, investments, market opportunities and interventions for priority commodities and landscapes, with a particular focus on implementation and delivery.

Key responsibilities include:

- Translate identified private sector opportunities into concrete partnership concepts, action plans, investment cases, market interventions, business models and implementation arrangements;

- Support market readiness and capacity development of smallholder producers, MSMEs and producer organizations to meet buyer requirements, sustainability standards, certification requirements and emerging international due diligence expectations;

- Identify and facilitate opportunities to mobilize private capital, commercial finance, impact investment, blended finance and other financing mechanisms for sustainable commodity production, value-chain development and landscape restoration;

- Identify, develop, pilot and scale innovative business models, financing mechanisms, digital solutions and market-based approaches that improve the sustainability, resilience and profitability of commodity value chains;

- Provide technical coordination and quality assurance for interventions related to sustainable trade, market access, certification recognition, responsible sourcing, international market engagement and market partnerships, in accordance with approved workplans and results frameworks;

- Identify synergies between projects and initiatives, particularly where investments, business models, market transformation and commodity interventions can be combined to improve scalability, sustainability and market viability;

- Coordinate with relevant UNDP teams and project personnel to develop public-private-community partnerships that support sustainable and resilient production, diversified livelihoods, conservation, restoration and responsible commodity development;

- Monitor the delivery and results of private sector partnerships, including progress on investment, co-financing, market access, sourcing commitments, producer engagement, certification, responsible sourcing and other agreed partnership outcomes.

Engagement on Policy, Regulatory and Market-Enabling Issues

The incumbent will provide technical analysis and facilitate dialogue between government, private sector and other stakeholders on policy, regulatory and market requirements affecting sustainable commodity production and trade.

Key responsibilities include:

- Maintain effective coordination and engagement with relevant government counterparts, including the Coordinating Ministry for Food Affairs, Ministry of Agriculture, Ministry of Forestry, BAPPENAS and other relevant national and sub-national institutions;

- Monitor and analyze emerging international trade, sustainability, certification and supply-chain due diligence requirements affecting Indonesian commodities;

- Provide technical analysis and advice on relevant international market and regulatory developments, including the European Union Deforestation Regulation (EUDR), UK forest-risk commodity requirements, supply-chain due diligence requirements, international sustainability standards and other relevant market regulations;

- Support the preparation of technical guidance, market assessments, policy briefs, analytical reports and recommendations to strengthen regulatory preparedness, supply-chain due diligence, sustainable trade and market access.

- Facilitate policy dialogue and knowledge exchange among government institutions, private sector actors, commodity associations, certification bodies, international buyers, financial institutions and development partners on sustainable commodity trade and market requirements;

- Support initiatives aimed at strengthening international recognition and market acceptance of Indonesian sustainability and certification systems, including ISPO and other relevant national standards;

- Where relevant and authorized, facilitate bilateral and multi-stakeholder dialogue between government and private sector actors, including dialogue related to market access, sustainable trade, certification recognition and international market requirements.

Contribution to UNDP Indonesia’s Overall Portfolio, Knowledge and Results

- Contribute actively to UNDP Indonesia’s overall private sector engagement strategy and identify opportunities to connect individual project partnerships with broader country-level priorities and investment opportunities;

- Identify opportunities to leverage public funding and UNDP resources to mobilize private sector capital, technical expertise, market access and co-financing;

- Facilitate knowledge exchange and cross-learning across FOLUR, FAST, SLPI, GCP and other relevant initiatives, particularly on private sector engagement, sustainable value chains, financing, responsible sourcing, market access and sustainable trade;

- Provide technical advice and strategic support to project teams, consultants and implementing partners on private sector engagement, market development, sustainable value chains and partnership implementation;

